+# The Daily Grind
+
+The homepage includes Grind Rewards registration using browser-assisted Email Verification (EVP). No passcode, magic link, or other verification email is sent. Accounts are created only after the server validates email ownership.
+
+Requires Node.js 24 or newer. There are no third-party runtime dependencies. `npm run build` performs syntax checks and automated tests without starting a server. Tests exercise application modules directly and never import `server.mjs`, open a listening socket, or launch a browser.
+
+For deployment, use the `npm start` command with `APP_ORIGIN` set to the exact public HTTPS origin (no trailing slash), `NODE_ENV=production`, and `DATABASE_PATH` pointing at a persistent writable volume. The start command is for deployment; it was not run during implementation. The default local origin is `http://localhost:11222`. Environment variables must be supplied by the hosting environment; `.env.example` documents them. The server does not automatically load `.env` files.
+
+## Browser verification setup
+
+The Email Verification API is experimental and currently requires Chrome's Email Verification origin trial. Register the deployed origin and set `EMAIL_VERIFICATION_ORIGIN_TRIAL_TOKEN`; the server includes it in the `Origin-Trial` response header. HTTPS, a participating browser and email provider, an active email-provider session in the same browser, and the customer's consent are necessary. No token was supplied in this checkout. Unsupported or denied verification fails clearly and leaves the customer unregistered; there is deliberately no email-based fallback.
+
+The server renders a fresh cryptographically random nonce into the hidden `autocomplete="email-verification-token"` input. Keep form submission native: the browser inserts its combined `<EVT>~<KB-JWT>` proof during submission. The form POSTs to `/api/rewards/signup`. Clients sending `Accept: application/json` get structured results; the homepage uses cookie-backed flash messages and a redirect. A static-only host can display the design but cannot register accounts.
+
+The endpoint enforces same-origin requests, body and rate limits, field validation, and acceptance of the terms. Challenges expire after ten minutes, are tied to an HttpOnly SameSite cookie, and are consumed atomically before asynchronous verification. The server verifies both JWT signatures, types, allowed asymmetric algorithms, exact email, verified flag, timestamp freshness, optional expiry, origin audience, nonce, and the hash linking the two JWTs. Issuer trust comes from the email domain's `_email-verification` DNS TXT record. Metadata identity is checked before fetching JWKS. Outbound requests use HTTPS, bounded responses, timeouts, no redirects, and pinned public IPv4 addresses to prevent SSRF and DNS rebinding. IPv6-only issuers are currently unsupported. The Chrome trial's older EdDSA identifier is accepted only with Ed25519 keys alongside fully specified Ed25519, ES256, and RS256.
+
+SQLite stores memberships, verification time and issuer, consent preferences, and accepted terms version. Signed proofs are not persisted or logged. Repeat sign-ups cannot modify an existing account. This change creates rewards memberships, not a general account authentication or rewards redemption system. Deploy one server against a persistent database; multiple replicas require shared transactional storage. Peer-address rate limiting intentionally ignores untrusted proxy headers; a reverse proxy may need a trusted rate-limit configuration.
+
+## References
+
+- [Chrome Email Verification guidance](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/identity/email-verification)
+- [Browser Email Verification API](https://wicg.github.io/email-verification/)
+- [Email Verification Protocol draft](https://dickhardt.github.io/email-verification/draft-hardt-email-verification.html)
+
+Stock photography is served from Unsplash. Existing menu, location, and contact destinations are retained from the original homepage. Rewards terms and registration privacy information are included at `/rewards-terms` and `/privacy-policy`.
